Choice: ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SORTING EXPERIMENT (key = name, case-insensitive)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  records                     : 10
  n * log2(n)                 : 33 (lower bound for comparison sorts)
  std::sort       comparisons : 22
  std::stable_sort comparisons: 22
  both copied the source first, so input was already in ID order.

  Result order (ID : Name)
    sort        : 1 2 3 8 9 7 5 4 10 6
    stable_sort : 1 2 3 8 9 7 5 4 10 6
  records sharing a name here: 1 pair(s)
  Note: both orders matched this time. That is luck, not a guarantee:
        std::sort may reorder records that share a name. See the stress test.

  Stress test on 64 synthetic records with 8 repeated names:
    std::sort        reordered 95 pair(s) of records that share a name
    std::stable_sort reordered 0 pair(s) of records that share a name
    std::sort + (name, id) key  reordered 0 pair(s) of records that share a name
    -> equal keys swap position under std::sort, so records can appear to
       jump around between runs. Two fixes: std::stable_sort, or break the
       tie in the comparator so the key is a total order. Both give 0.
  Sorted by name:
    #1   Aarav Sharma           98765 43210
    #2   Ananya Iyer            98123 45678
